---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import StatisticsCalculator from '@components/islands/StatisticsCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('math', 'standard-deviation-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is standard deviation?',
    answer:
      'Standard deviation measures how spread out a set of numbers is around the mean. A low value means the data points cluster close to the average; a high value means they are more dispersed. It is the square root of the variance, expressed in the same units as the data.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate standard deviation step by step?',
    answer:
      'Find the mean of the data; subtract the mean from each value and square the result; add up those squared differences; divide by N for a population or N−1 for a sample to get the variance; then take the square root. That square root is the standard deviation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between sample and population standard deviation?',
    answer:
      'Population standard deviation (σ) divides by N and is used when your data is the entire group. Sample standard deviation (s) divides by N−1 (Bessel\'s correction) and is used when your data is a sample estimating a larger population — it gives a less biased estimate and is the more common case.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate standard deviation in Excel?',
    answer:
      'Use =STDEV.S(range) for a sample or =STDEV.P(range) for a population, pointing at the cells that hold your numbers. The matching variance functions are =VAR.S and =VAR.P, and =AVERAGE gives the mean. This calculator shows all of them at once so you can check your spreadsheet.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is variance?',
    answer:
      'Variance is the average of the squared differences from the mean — standard deviation before you take the square root. It is in squared units, which is why standard deviation (the square root) is usually easier to interpret.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What counts as a high or low standard deviation?',
    answer:
      'It is relative to the mean and the context. The same standard deviation can be large for small numbers and tiny for large ones, so analysts often compare the coefficient of variation (standard deviation ÷ mean) to judge spread across different data sets.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Calculate the population and sample standard deviation, variance and mean of any data set — with the full working, plus the median, quartiles and five-number summary."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }}
>
  <StatisticsCalculator primary="sd" />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to calculate standard deviation, step by step</h2>
    <p>Standard deviation looks intimidating but it is five plain steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the <strong>mean</strong> (average) of your numbers.</li>
      <li>Subtract the mean from each value to get its <strong>deviation</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Square</strong> each deviation.</li>
      <li>Add the squares and divide — by <strong>N</strong> for a population, or <strong>N−1</strong> for a sample — to get the <strong>variance</strong>.</li>
      <li>Take the <strong>square root</strong> of the variance. That is the standard deviation.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Paste your numbers into the calculator — separated by commas, spaces or line breaks — and it runs all five steps for both the population and sample cases at once.</p>

    <h2>What standard deviation tells you</h2>
    <p>
      Standard deviation measures how far a set of numbers typically sits from its mean. A <strong>low</strong> value means the data clusters tightly around the average; a <strong>high</strong> value means it is spread out. Because it is the square root of the variance, it is expressed in the <em>same units as your data</em> — dollars, centimetres, seconds — which makes it far easier to interpret than the variance itself.
    </p>

    <h2>The formulas</h2>
    <p>Both versions start from the same sum of squared deviations and differ only in the divisor:</p>
    <p><strong>Population: σ = √( Σ(x − μ)<sup>2</sup> / N )</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Sample: s = √( Σ(x − x̄)<sup>2</sup> / (N − 1) )</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>x</strong> — each value in the data set</li>
      <li><strong>μ</strong> / <strong>x̄</strong> — the mean (μ for a population, x̄ for a sample)</li>
      <li><strong>N</strong> — the count of values</li>
      <li><strong>Σ(x − mean)<sup>2</sup></strong> — the sum of every squared deviation; drop the square root and this over the divisor is the <strong>variance</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>Take the data <strong>2, 4, 6, 8</strong>. The mean is (2 + 4 + 6 + 8) ÷ 4 = 5. The deviations are −3, −1, 1, 3, and their squares are 9, 1, 1, 9, which sum to 20. From there:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Population</strong>: variance = 20 ÷ 4 = 5, so σ = √5 ≈ <strong>2.236</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Sample</strong>: variance = 20 ÷ 3 ≈ 6.667, so s = √6.667 ≈ <strong>2.582</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The sample figure is a little larger because dividing by N−1 instead of N inflates it slightly to correct for sampling.</p>

    <h2>Population (σ) vs. sample (s)</h2>
    <p>Use the <strong>population</strong> standard deviation when your data is the whole group you care about. Use the <strong>sample</strong> standard deviation when your data is a sample used to estimate a larger population — the more common case in research. The only difference is dividing by N versus N−1 (Bessel's correction); the calculator shows both so you never have to choose blind.</p>

    <h2>Is a standard deviation high or low?</h2>
    <p>
      There is no universal threshold — the same value can be large for small numbers and trivial for large ones. To compare spread across data sets on different scales, analysts use the <strong>coefficient of variation</strong>, the standard deviation divided by the mean. For the sample above that is 2.582 ÷ 5 ≈ 0.52, or about 52%, telling you the spread is roughly half the size of the average value.
    </p>

    <h2>Standard deviation in Excel or Sheets</h2>
    <p>To reproduce these in a spreadsheet, point the functions at your data range: <strong>=STDEV.S(A1:A20)</strong> for a sample, <strong>=STDEV.P(A1:A20)</strong> for a population, with <strong>=VAR.S</strong>/<strong>=VAR.P</strong> for variance and <strong>=AVERAGE</strong> for the mean. The results here match those functions, so it doubles as a quick way to check a spreadsheet.</p>
    <p>For the median, mode, quartiles and the rest of the descriptive picture, use the fuller <a href="/math/statistics-calculator">statistics calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
